#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""The Samba Active Directory write helper for Newton (SPEC-1.4-REQUEST).

The helper runs on the Samba domain controller as root.  It is the write
half of the 1.4 contract: the read half is the collector, and this one
changes the directory under a signed approval and puts the change back
when the approval never comes.

It is a standalone script: it imports nothing of Newton, so it can be
installed on a DC that knows nothing about the Newton machine.  It reads
one JSON request on stdin, at most 64 KiB, and writes one JSON object on
stdout.  It exits 0 whatever the outcome; a non-zero exit means the
helper itself crashed.

The only other way in is the undo, ``--undo <txid>``, and it is refused
when ``SUDO_USER`` is set: the undo runs from the timer of systemd, never
through the SSH channel of the write login.

The three files of section 8 are read before anything else happens: the
key of the approval, the name of the domain device and the keytab of the write
account.  Each one is opened without following a link and each one is
checked with ``fstat`` on the descriptor of the open file: the file must
be a regular file of root, with the mode the spec names.  The install is
checked the way the 1.3 collector checks its own: the file of the helper,
the three files of the configuration and every directory of the install,
up to the root of the filesystem, must be owned by root and must not be
writable by their group or by the others.  The check runs before the
samba bindings are imported.

The write account is never used with a password: the helper takes a
ticket from the keytab of the DC with ``kinit -k`` into a private
credential cache under ``/run/newton-ad``, and it binds over LDAP with
that cache.  The credentials are the measured ones of section 9: the
realm, the workgroup, the kerberos state and the named cache.  The
guess of the credentials is never called: it would leave the principal
as root and the bind would fail.

The directory is read through the samba bindings and never through
``ldbsearch``: the LDIF that ``ldbsearch`` writes rewrites SIDs and
security descriptors as text.  The bind is the measured one of section 9:
``SamDB(url="ldap://<fqdn>", credentials=..., lp=...)``, where the fqdn
is the netbios name of the host and the realm, both lowercased.

Every write is the measured atomic modify: one message with two elements,
the first deleting the exact old value and the second adding the new one.
The name of the object is in the message, the way the bindings take it.
A stale old value raises 16 (a conflict), a write outside the delegation
raises 50 (a denial) and a missing object raises 32.

The privileged set is the one of section 5.  It is walked here and not
with the matching rule ``1.2.840.113556.1.4.1941``, which is unmeasured
on Samba: a breadth first walk of the ``memberOf`` values with a visited
set and a depth cap of 32.  The primary group of an object is one of its
memberships and it is included.

The risk of the request is derived again here, from the live object, and
a request whose risk is not the one the helper derives is refused with
``risk_mismatch``: the owner approves the level the helper sees.

The transaction store is ``/var/lib/newton-ad/tx``.  Every mode but
``read`` holds an exclusive lock on it for the whole run, and the
start-up sweep takes the same lock: every run undoes the armed records
whose deadline has passed.  The sweep never confirms anything.

The secret of a user-create is in the request and in nothing else: it is
in no record, in no answer and in no line of the log.
"""

